      <p class="lead"><strong>Every EMF number currently circulating for infrared saunas — including the "third-party tested" ones — comes from a test commissioned by the brand selling the sauna.</strong> Nobody publishes independent, cross-brand EMF data with a stated, replicable methodology. This page explains why that's a problem, and lays out the methodology we're using to fix it.</p>

      <div class="call evidence">
        <div class="h">&#10003; Why this page exists</div>
        <p>AI Overview answers for EMF-related sauna queries now explicitly tell readers to "look for independent, third-party testing" — while citing only manufacturer-commissioned reports as if they qualified. That's not a fringe complaint; it's what the current AI-generated answers say, right before pointing you at the exact kind of source they just warned you about. This page is our attempt to actually be the source they're describing.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="problem">The problem with every EMF number you've seen</h2>
      <p class="sub">A 65x spread for products all marketed as "low EMF."</p>

      <p>Search "infrared sauna EMF levels" and you'll find claims ranging from 0.3 mG to over 20 mG — a roughly 65x spread — for products all marketed as "low EMF." Nearly every specific number traces back to one of two places:</p>

      <ol>
        <li><strong>The manufacturer's own marketing copy</strong>, with no test report attached.</li>
        <li><strong>A "third-party test report,"</strong> almost always conducted by one of a small handful of EMF-testing labs (Vitatech Electromagnetics is the name that recurs most often), but <strong>commissioned and hosted by the sauna brand itself</strong> — Sunlighten, Good Health Saunas, and Vortex Saunas all publish their own brand-specific Vitatech PDF reports directly on their own sites.</li>
      </ol>

      <p>A test being conducted by a real lab with real equipment doesn't make it independent if the brand being tested chose when to test, which unit to test, and whether to publish the results. Nobody currently tests across brands, with a consistent protocol, and publishes results regardless of outcome — favorable or not.</p>

      <h2 id="low-emf">What "low EMF" actually means (and doesn't)</h2>
      <p class="sub">It's not a regulated term.</p>

      <p>"Low EMF" is not a regulated term. There's no certification body, no legal threshold, no standardized test position that every brand agrees to use. EMF inside a sauna cabin is really three separate physical phenomena:</p>

      <ul>
        <li><strong>Magnetic field</strong> — generated by current running through heater wiring, measured in milligauss (mG)</li>
        <li><strong>Electric field</strong> — generated around energized conductors, measured in volts per meter (V/m)</li>
        <li><strong>RF (radiofrequency)</strong> — from the sauna's controller electronics and any wireless/Bluetooth module</li>
      </ul>

      <p>Most of the field a person actually sits in comes from wiring layout and controller design — not from the infrared heating element itself. That means "low EMF" is fundamentally a claim about electrical engineering quality, not about the type of heater installed. A sticker that says "Low EMF Certified" tells you almost nothing without a specific number, a specific measurement position, and a specific meter.</p>

      <p>International safety bodies (ICNIRP) set public exposure limits far above what any sauna produces — around 2,000 mG for magnetic fields at 60Hz. Every infrared sauna on the market, even "high EMF" budget models, tests well under that regulatory ceiling. The debate isn't really about regulatory safety; it's about the wide gap between what's regulated as legal and what precautionary, building-biology-oriented guidance recommends (often under 1-3 mG for prolonged seated exposure) — and about whether the number printed in a brand's marketing actually matches what a buyer's unit produces once it arrives.</p>

      <h2 id="methodology">Our testing methodology</h2>
      <p class="sub">Published in full so it can be checked, criticized, and replicated.</p>

      <p>This is the protocol we're using, published in full so it can be checked, criticized, and replicated by anyone — including competitors.</p>

      <div class="proto">
        <div class="pstep"><div><b>Meter and calibration</b><div class="t">Instrument transparency</div>We test with a meter calibrated against manufacturer specifications within the prior 12 months. We report the meter model and calibration date alongside every result.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><b>Measurement position</b><div class="t">Where you actually sit</div>Every reading is taken at seated chest height, at the actual seating position a user occupies during a session — not at the panel surface, not on the floor. Panel-surface and floor readings produce dramatically different (and misleadingly low) numbers.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><b>What we measure</b><div class="t">Three components, reported separately</div>All three components — magnetic field (mG), electric field (V/m), and RF where a wireless module is present — reported separately, not blended into a single composite "EMF score."</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><b>Sample size</b><div class="t">Manufacturing variance matters</div>Wherever possible, we test more than one unit of the same model, since manufacturing variance between individual units is one of the least-discussed sources of the range in claimed numbers. Single-unit results are labeled as such.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><b>Brand independence</b><div class="t">No conditioned outcomes</div>We do not accept sauna units, payment, or review copies conditioned on outcome from any brand. Where a brand provides a unit for testing, that's disclosed directly next to the result.</div></div>
        <div class="pstep"><div><b>Publication policy</b><div class="t">Every result, favorable or not</div>Every unit tested gets published — favorable or not. A brand's numbers don't get held back if they underperform their own marketing claims.</div></div>
      </div>

