      <h2 id="how-to-read">How to read any EMF claim you encounter elsewhere</h2>
      <p class="sub">Four questions to ask before trusting any number.</p>

      <p>Until independent, cross-brand data is more widely available, here's how to evaluate any EMF number you see:</p>

      <ul class="mistakes">
        <li><b>Ask who commissioned the test.</b> If the brand being tested paid for and published the report, treat the number as marketing, not verification — regardless of how credible the testing lab's name sounds.</li>
        <li><b>Ask where the meter was positioned.</b> A number measured at the panel surface or on the floor is not the number that matters. Ask specifically for seated-position, chest-height readings.</li>
        <li><b>Ask for the meter model and calibration date.</b> A number with no stated instrument or calibration is not verifiable.</li>
        <li><b>Be skeptical of round, marketing-friendly numbers</b> ("0.3 mG!") presented with no methodology attached. Real measurements vary by unit, position, and even proximity to other electronics in the room.</li>
      </ul>

